DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T143000
SUMMARY:What We Can Know by Ian McEwan [OPTION 1]
DESCRIPTION:Novel Lovers of Sonoma County\, CA\n***What We Can Know* by Ia
 n McEwan**\n\n**Ed's description**: "It is 2119\, and Thomas Metcalfe is a
  humanities academic expert on the 90 30 era - 1990 to 2030 - when the Der
 angement accelerated leading eventually to the Inundation which destroyed 
 much of the civilized world with ephemeral archives preserved in mountaino
 us libraries while Nigeria houses ALL digital records. Thomas embarks on a
  hero's journey to find the long lost but legendary 'Corona for Vivien' re
 cited by the famed poet Francis Blundy for his wife's fiftieth birthday pa
 rty at an 'immortal night' with literary luminaries in 2014. In this perio
 d the STEM colleges have all the best students and facilities while Humani
 ties colleges have the lousiest students and facilities\, and Thomas hopes
  his finding of the corona - a poetic structure usually of seven sonnets b
 ut 15 in this case - will spark some life in his dying field. He finds Viv
 ien's memoirs\, and the second part of the novel belongs to her."\n\n**Luc
 inda**: "This book was a mind bender. I never quite knew what was going on
  until the very end. I believe that’s the whole point. I appreciated McE
 wan’s views on climate decline and what it might lead to. It was thought
  provoking (no spoilers). I also found the book a little hard to read as i
 t was quite detailed\, and the characters might be considered amoral. Ther
 e is a lot here."\n\n**Dave**: "Intriguing novel. Pondering the future not
  about technological change but what will do with the vast amount of saved
  data. How will we encounter our history and the place for humanities in t
 he future."\n\n**Nancy R**: "I've started on my second reading. There's so
  much in the book to discuss. One thing is 100 years in the future calling
  global warming 'the derangement.' brilliant\, among others. The story is 
 involving and well written\, and is sure to generate lively discussions."\
 n\n*•* "Sophisticated entertainment of a high order." —*The New York T
 imes*\n*• "*Brilliantly\, and surprisingly\, plotted."—*The Washington
  Post •* "A novelist of consummate skill."—*The Wall Street Journal*\n
 *•* "Elegantly structured and provocative."—*Los Angeles Times*
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/novel-lovers-of-sebastopol/events/316
 144129/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260816T182502Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260816T182502Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
