SUMMARY:Whistler by Ann Patchett [OPTION 1]
DESCRIPTION:Novel Lovers of Sonoma County\, CA\n***Whistler*** by Ann Patc
 hett. If you rely on library copies\, reserve ASAP as there's a long wait 
 list. \\*\\**We're scheduling this book early in the month - 2nd and 3rd S
 at. - to avoid Thanksgiving weekend.\\*\\**\n\nPUBLISHER'S DESCRIPTION: "W
 hen Daphne Fuller and her husband Jonathan visit the Metropolitan Museum o
 f Art\, they notice an older\, white-haired gentleman following them. The 
 man turns out to be Eddie Triplett\, her former stepfather\, who had been 
 married to her mother for a little more than year when Daphne was nine. No
 w 53\, Daphne hasn’t seen Eddie for many years\, not since the fateful e
 vent that changed the direction of both their lives. Meeting again\, time 
 falls away\; while their relationship was brief\, it had a profound impact
  on them both\, and now that they are reunited\, they have no intention of
  ever being separated again.\n\n"*Whistler* is a story about two adults lo
 oking back over the choices they made\, and the choices that were made for
  them. It’s a story about bravery\, memory\, the often small yet consequ
 ential moments that define our lives\, and the endless stream of loss that
  in time comes for us all. Beautiful in its simplicity\, it is ultimately 
 about how love endures\, and how the feeling of being known by one other p
 erson\, even for a short period of time\, can change everything."\n\n"This
  exquisite writer has once again delivered an incandescent work of fiction
  — sweet\, but never sentimental\, infinitely wise and suffused with lov
 e. It’s also an ode to New York City itself…. While Patchett has clear
 ly drawn on actual events and individuals to produce this luminous work\, 
 she exhibits the expert novelist’s knack for following a plot where the 
 imagination takes it.... I didn't want it to end." - Leigh Haber\, ***The 
 Los Angeles Times***\n\n“Ann Patchett’s new novel is a rare phenomenon
  in contemporary fiction: a novel both majestic and intimate\, original an
 d masterful in its structure\, crystalline in its prose\, revelatory in it
 s insights\, utterly devastating yet ultimately uplifting in its emotional
  impact. . . . I think it is her best novel yet.” - Priscilla Gilman\, *
 **The Boston Globe***\n\n"Ann Patchett’s new novel\, *Whistler*\, is tha
 t loveliest of summer gifts\, a story of reconciliation\, of old affection
 s renewed\, of a family’s circumference enlarged. Fans of the author’s
  previous work will open these pages like an old shoebox of family photos.
  Newcomers — how I envy them those first moments — will discover a wri
 ter of gentle wit and quiet grace." - **Ron Charles**\n\n**Christine**: "J
 ust finished listening to this book and absolutely loved it! If you are in
 terested\, please listen to the Audible\, read by Patchett\, who is an exc
 ellent narrator. I've already recommended it to a number of friends. BIG t
 humbs' up! Would definitely read it again."\n\n**Nancy S**: "I liked *Whis
 tler* a lot. Mostly it hit some personal spots with characters just like p
 eople I know and situations I have been in. It is Anne Patchett in her mos
 t comfortable place— family type relationships\, reevaluated\, and trans
 formed and just about always for the better."
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/novel-lovers-of-sebastopol/events/316
 144717/
